# Chain: Swarm (SW1)

ID: SW1
Parent: D0

Purpose:
  The parallel micro-unit delivery chain with a defect-focus loop.
  Macro-sequential, micro-parallel. Each stage runs a SWARM cycle
  (RECONCILE -> DECOMPOSE -> DISPATCH -> MERGE -> VERIFY -> DEFECT-CHECK
  -> FOCUS LOOP -> Stage DONE) inside a pyramid of recursive sub-agents
  with dual dispatch channels (R ordered / F free). DL1 is untouched and
  immutable.

Owns: chains/swarm.sdd

Flow:
  DB -> DB-TS -> BE -> BE-TS -> API -> API-TS -> FE -> FE-TS ->
  UNIT -> UNIT-TS -> E2E -> E2E-TS -> FULL -> RF-G -> FB -> REL

FlowNotation:
  short: DB -> DB-TS -> BE -> BE-TS -> API -> API-TS -> FE -> FE-TS ->
    UNIT -> UNIT-TS -> E2E -> E2E-TS -> FULL -> RF-G -> FB -> REL
  canonical_keys: 16 (bax ../workflow/step-keys.sdd SW1 group)

Dependencies:
  DB-TS  -> [DB]
  BE     -> [DB-TS]
  BE-TS  -> [BE]
  API    -> [BE-TS]
  API-TS -> [API]
  FE     -> [API-TS]
  FE-TS  -> [FE]
  UNIT   -> [FE-TS]
  UNIT-TS -> [UNIT]
  E2E    -> [UNIT-TS]
  E2E-TS -> [E2E]
  FULL   -> [E2E-TS]
  RF-G   -> [FULL]
  FB     -> [RF-G]
  REL    -> [FB]

Parallelism:
  Within each stage, independent micro-units (PARALLEL_READY, no shared
  mutable state) are dispatched concurrently to subagents. Stages are
  strictly sequential — a stage's micro-units must all be DONE before
  the next stage starts. Human gates (REL approval) are NOT parallelized.

ImmutabilityRules:
  [SW1-1] The stage order is IMMUTABLE — no skipping, no reordering.
  [SW1-2] RF-G is a GLOBAL RF instance: it runs once, after FULL, scoped
          per RFScopedSemantics (stages.sdd). It is NOT an in-branch RF.
  [SW1-3] Every /sdd-swarm invocation continues from SAVED STATE, never
          from conversation memory.
  [SW1-4] A stage is DONE only when: all its micro-units are DONE, the
          stage's VERIFY passed, and no open defect remains unattributed.
  [SW1-5] A blocking BUG stops the stage and the flow (bax
          ../tasks/BUG-REGISTRY.sdd).
  [SW1-6] REL is the terminal stage and requires human approval ([CMD3],
          [RS1]) — it is never auto-skipped.
  [SW1-7] DL1 is untouched by SW1. SW1 is a separate chain with its own
          StepState; no changes to chains/delivery.sdd.
  [SW1-8] FLOW VERSIONING: persisted runs whose state carries no
           flow_version are v1 runs and are DEPRECATED — /sdd-swarm MUST
           re-initialize the run, never resume a v1 run.
  [SW1-9] PYRAMID DEPTH CAP: spawn depth is capped at 2 levels below
           the supervisor (L0 supervisor -> L1 stage-agent -> L2
           sub-agent). An L2 agent spawns nothing (3+ supervisor levels
           are an anti-pattern); over-budget work at L2 returns PARTIAL
           with a subdivision note.
  [SW1-10] Sub-agents follow the lifecycle in
           ../agent/subagent-lifecycle.sdd: self-delegated spawn,
           single-writer scope, typed outcome written to STATE, runtime
           folder, state-driven triggers.
  [SW1-11] RECONCILE runs before DISPATCH on every /sdd-swarm
           invocation. New dispatch is BLOCKED until the
           sub-agent registry is clean or the human explicitly waives.
  [SW1-12] Dispatch channels are within-stage only: Channel F is free
           parallelism inside the CURRENT stage; Channel R is
           dependency-ordered inside the CURRENT stage. No channel
           dispatches across a stage boundary ([SW1-1]).
  [SW1-13] An agent folder is deleted only when its unit is DONE +
           VERIFIED with no open attributed defect; reconciliation
           prefers re-verify over destructive cleanup.
  [SW1-14] All final test execution runs inside Docker ([R102], [W19])
           — never on the host. This includes stage, full-system, and
           E2E runs recorded as done_proof.

SWARMCycle (per stage):
   0. RECONCILE — sub-agent chain check before any dispatch: read
      sub_agent_registry[], verify parent_id ownership per entry,
      re-verify or defect-catalog orphaned RUNNING entries, delete stale
      folders. Dispatch is BLOCKED until the registry is clean or the
      human waives (bax ../agent/subagent-lifecycle.sdd Reconciliation).
   1. DECOMPOSE — stage scope -> micro-units (MU-XXX). Build dependency
      DAG (no cycles); mark independent units PARALLEL_READY.
   2. DISPATCH — each unit routes to exactly one dispatch channel
      (bax ../workflow/dispatch-channels.sdd): channel = F iff
      parallel_ready && shared_state == none, else R. Channel F units
      dispatch concurrently within the CURRENT stage only (work-stealing
      across free slots); Channel R units wait for their depends_on set
      to reach DONE. The supervisor dispatches; each L1 agent subdivides
      and spawns L2 sub-agents itself when a unit is over-budget
      (bax ../agent/subagent-lifecycle.sdd). Each agent reassembles
      context via the handoff protocol — context is NOT copied
      (@agent/handoff.sdd).
   3. MERGE — fan-in; conflicts detected and resolved ([P4]); failed
      units isolated ([P5]).
   4. VERIFY — stage test cases run inside Docker ([R102], [I23-I28]);
      result recorded per unit.
   5. DEFECT-CHECK — supervisor scans results: test failure, merge
      conflict, quality finding -> defect catalog (DEF-XXX).
   6. FOCUS LOOP — each defect attributed to exactly one MU-XXX; narrow
      context re-dispatch + re-verify. Bounds: arm cap 10, error spiral
      3, LOOP_SCORE >= 60 -> strategy change, >= 90 -> escalate
      ([LP11-LP13], [SUP-03]).
   7. Stage DONE -> close ladder check (CloseLadder below) -> next
      macro-stage. A dependent unit of the next stage becomes
      dispatchable only through this DONE transition — state-driven,
      never message-driven.

CloseLadder (DEC -> TASK -> SUBTASK -> AGENT fan-in):
  level_1_subtask: a MICRO-UNIT (SUBTASK) closes on a typed SUCCESS
    outcome plus done_proof ([MU-4]) — never on a natural-language claim.
  level_2_task: a TASK closes when ALL of its micro-units are DONE, the
    business-case analysis passes (BDD scenarios vs acceptance criteria),
    and the stage tests pass inside Docker ([R102], [W19]).
  level_3_dec: a DEC closes when all its tasks are DONE, the full-system
    tests pass (E2E / E2E-TS / FULL), and business validation succeeds —
    then the decision is closed and the next stage or decision becomes
    dispatchable.
  rule: no level skips a lower level — a MU that is not DONE keeps its
    TASK open; a TASK that is not closed keeps its DEC open.

FocusLoop:
  attribution: defect -> MU-XXX (one defect, one unit)
  context_pack: defect + unit + BEFORE/CHANGE/AFTER (narrow, <= 8k tokens)
  bounds:
    arm_cap: 10          # max focus iterations per stage
    error_spiral: 3      # 3 consecutive typed failures -> wrap/escalate
    loop_score_high: 60  # strategy change required
    loop_score_crit: 90  # escalate to human
  record: focus_iterations[] appended to state with reason + evidence
    delta per [SP4].

StepState (persisted per swarm run):
  storage: .specdd/swarm/{run_id}/state.sdd (runtime-only, [R103])
  fields: run_id, flow_version (2), current_stage, stage_states[],
    micro_units[], defects[], focus_iterations[], token_totals,
    loop_guards, last_checkpoint,
    sub_agent_registry[], channel_routing[], folders[]
  sub_agent_registry[]: { agent_id, parent_id, mu_id, level,
    spawned_at } — one entry per spawned agent; parent_id ownership is
    verified at RECONCILE ([SW1-11]).
  channel_routing[]: { mu_id, channel, reason, agent_id,
    dispatched_at } — one entry per dispatch ([DC4]).
  folders[]: { agent_id, path, status } — runtime agent folders under
    .specdd/swarm/{run_id}/agents/{agent_id}/; deleted only after
    DONE + VERIFIED ([SW1-13]).
  token_totals: <per-unit and per-stage aggregates ([I10] extension)>

Retry: 3

